# Alladi Struggles

Tamil title: அல்லாடுகிறார்

Imagine the struggle involved if a blind thief in a dark room tried to find and retrieve a black egg hidden inside a rolled sleeping mat. How much wandering and struggling must a lawyer endure if, with brutish obstinacy, he clings to a barren doctrine and insists upon advancing a perverse argument? However strenuously a lawyer may argue that iron is a splinter and a bitter substance is sugarcane, can the argument stand or obtain a verdict if the listener possesses even a little intelligence? If the case is one thing and the argument another, how can the case end or a judgment be obtained?

We feel great pity on seeing Sir Alladi Krishnaswami Ayyar caught in just such a difficulty and struggling. Advocate-General Alladi has received a case and tried to display his skill in arguing it.

## Alladi's Peculiar Argument

Some days ago, at the Maharaja's College Union in Ernakulam, Sir Alladi spoke about one of today's most important and intimidating questions: partition. He attacked both the Pakistan proposal and the proposal to separate Dravida Nadu. Declaring that partition must not occur, he elaborated many peculiar arguments.

Alladi said that a resurgence of races had arisen in India, that distinctions were being spoken of between a Muslim race and a Hindu race, and that this ought not to happen. He said many things in support of his argument.

Rather than helping him, Sir Alladi's argument supports those demanding partition. Partition does not mean splitting a single, uniform and disciplined society. India's many races were gathered and mixed together; that mixture proved fruitless. One race became the maker of public affairs, the all-knowing authority in society, the broker in religion and the teacher in education, suppressing and destroying the other races. This horror occurred in the past and continues even now.

The present question is how to stop it and enable every race to preserve its natural character, govern itself and live. The existing "joint system" opposes this. Under it, one section labours and another consumes the fruit of that labour. Many races thereby lose their own character and, finding no worldly happiness, become trapped in suffering and decay. If this cruelty is to end, the bond by which one race has tied down the others must be cut.

There would be no division if everyone's property were genuinely common property. Instead, one race claims that the property of every race belongs to itself and acts accordingly. On the pretext that all belong to one family, it drinks the juice and awards the refuse to the others. Therefore a partition suit has been filed.

## The Partition Suit

When a partition suit is filed, the court need consider only whether those claiming division possess a right in the property to be divided. Nothing else is relevant.

A preacher may deliver sermons on unity and say, "What does it matter who rules or who enjoys the property?" It is only the listener who bears the hardship, and so sermons come rolling forth in abundance.

A person of judgment, rather than a preacher, would ask, "Why do you seek partition?" The plaintiffs would explain: "These cruelties exist; this much loss and hardship arose from living together; and if I separate, I expect to obtain these benefits." After hearing the explanation, a sensible person might say, "I shall seek a way to remove these grievances; continue to live together."

But when a person entitled to property asks that his share be divided and handed over, no one can say that it is impossible or that he must not divide it. This is the alphabet of Hindu law, which the Advocate-General has studied and absorbed completely. Yet the Advocate-General opposes partition. Why?

## The Reason He Gives

"India is one country. Although its people possess many differences, all must be regarded as a single race. People now newly claim that Muslims are one race and Hindus another; this ought not to be done. Many races have already mingled, and it is impossible to determine conclusively who belongs to which race.

"History teaches that in every country many races lived together and later merged into one. In Britain, for example, there were originally different races called Norman, Dane and Anglo-Saxon. Today a new common race called the British has arisen.

"We have long adhered to the principle that India is one country. Asoka and Akbar worked with this ideal in mind. India has a common culture. Politicians and others must therefore make unity, fellowship, discipline and national feeling grow, rather than cultivate the divisive sentiment that we are different from them."

We have presented Sir Alladi's argument at some length. Unlike Achariyar, he did not tell little tales asking whether one should slaughter a cow or cut Mother India into pieces. He tried to offer "arguments" that appear strong at first sight. We want the people to understand that, however skilful the opposing counsel may be, even if he is the Advocate-General, these are all the reasons he can produce against partition.

## Alladi Cannot Prevent Aryanism's Destruction

We often see this in life: when a disease is not cured by English, Ayurvedic or Unani medicines, the patient seeks a Siddha physician; when that physician brings sindooram, the patient also collects the addresses of astrologers and sorcerers; and after trying everything, he struggles and cries that the disease still has not gone. As the illness grows worse, it is customary to change doctors every hour.

Likewise, there is nothing surprising in Aryanism's search, from hour to hour, for different physicians to treat the dangerous disease that now afflicts it. But curry leaves cannot cure tuberculosis, and Alladi cannot prevent Aryanism's destruction.

We wish to ask the distinguished advocate Sir Alladi one thing. Under the law of partition known to him, are Muslims and Dravidians co-owners of the property called India, or are they not? Have they reached the age of majority at which they may claim their share, or have they not? Is there sufficient family conflict to demand division, or is there not? When such a share is demanded, does any court possess the power to say it cannot be given? Is there a law preventing partition?

Let Sir Alladi display a little of his forensic skill. If any legal authority exists, let him produce and extend it before us. Otherwise, let him remain in a corner, saying, "Why should I involve myself in this quarrel?" Once one ascends the forum, it is not proper to use evasion as a weapon instead of answering the arguments of the other side. Yet every one of Alladi's arguments is empty and hollow.

## A Sermon or Babbling?

He cannot deny that many races inhabit the country. If he does, history and the present condition of society will refute him. Alladi, who belongs to a race that migrated into this country, will not deny that Muslims and Dravidians are entitled to it. There is no law saying that what rightful owners ask for cannot be given, and he is learned in law. He therefore cannot prevent it. Instead, he offers a sermon. Because that sermon is useless, it is framed in a manner that makes angry listeners ask whether it is mere babbling.

He says that Britain once contained different classes just as this country does. True. But did the Saxon call the Englishman an untouchable? Did the Celt worship the Scot as a Brahmin? No. Were there laws and customs declaring it sinful for a Dane to drink water touched by a Norman? No. Did the Englishman live in an agraharam and the Saxon in a segregated cheri? Never. Did Brahma create some of them from his face and others from his back? No.

Britain contained many races and today they have become one because there was no distinction of high and low by birth. There existed the feeling that humanity was one community. Affection, kinship, unity and fellowship therefore grew. Later the fragrant flower called the British people blossomed, and its scent crossed the seas and spread across unseen lands.

Aryan and Dravidian races have existed here for thousands upon thousands of years. Has the distinction been removed? Muslim and Hindu communities have lived here for many centuries. Has the painful difference been abolished?

Even today we ask: what path exists for the idea that "all are one" to grow? What method is being employed? Where is the plan? Who is its leader? Can Alladi show us?

The philosophy that a country's people form one nation is nationalism. Sir Alladi says that, for this to exist, people living in one territory must possess a unified cultural consciousness, a common history and customs, and one purpose. That is a perfectly correct definition.

According to that definition, let Alladi examine Indian society. Have Aryan and Dravidian cultures become one? Have the histories of the two groups become one? Are their customs and purposes the same? Did Manu not construct his code of justice by placing the Dravidian alongside animal species such as the frog, snake, squirrel and cat? Is not Manu the weapon of Aryan domination? Does the "nationalism" described by Alladi exist?

Within our society, have Hindu customs and practices become the same as Muslim customs and practices? Can they be merged? People use the word "Hindu" to deceive, but do Brahmins and others occupy an equal position within it? No. Have all these races somehow united and begun living without conflict? Can anyone even claim that they have? No.

Then why should partition not be demanded? There is a right, there is grievance, there is law, and there is history. Why, therefore, must Sir Alladi stand in the way and shout? We ask only why Alladi struggles.

In the partition suit, the Advocate-General has neither law nor history to support his argument. The case is serious. The litigants tried speaking to the mediators without success. They lived together, suffered ruin and wandered in distress, and finally came to court. The Advocate-General has unnecessarily entered this case and now struggles. What a pity! What a pity!

*Viduthalai*, 18 July 1940.
